WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.615
Two fictional contacts arrive.

00:00:01.845 --> 00:00:08.658
A bank-looking text says a transfer is at risk
and asks the person to move money to a “safe
account” and reply with a one-time code.

00:00:08.888 --> 00:00:14.517
A second notice says a tax message is waiting
and tells the person to sign in through the
usual government route.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:18.680
A familiar logo or official-sounding name does
not identify the sender.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:24.196
Start by underlining the action each message
requests and decide whether it is reversible
or sensitive.

00:00:24.276 --> 00:00:29.435
Scams often create urgency, secrecy, threats,
or an unexpected reward.

00:00:29.665 --> 00:00:39.925
Requests to transfer money to a “safe
account,” buy gift cards, use cryptocurrency,
install remote-access software, or share a
password or one-time code are high-risk.

00:00:40.155 --> 00:00:44.438
Poor spelling can be a clue, but polished
writing does not prove a message is real.

00:00:44.668 --> 00:00:48.451
A cue is evidence to slow down, not a complete
identity test.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:53.051
Do not click, reply, transfer, or share
secrets while pressured.

00:00:53.131 --> 00:00:58.393
For a bank concern, open the known app or use
contact details from a bank card or statement.

00:00:58.623 --> 00:01:04.537
Do not call a number included only in the
unexpected text, and do not trust caller ID by
itself.

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:10.110
For a web notice, navigate to the known
official site yourself rather than following a
message link.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.772
If uncertain, talk to a trusted adult and ask
the institution through an independently found
route.

00:01:16.002 --> 00:01:20.969
A search result or advertisement may also be
misleading; use the known official domain.

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:28.112
Do not memorize the false rule “CRA never
calls.” The Canada Revenue Agency may contact
people by phone and other channels.

00:01:28.342 --> 00:01:32.578
A caller ID that displays a government name
still does not prove who is calling.

00:01:32.808 --> 00:01:45.577
If a person is unsure, they can ask for the
caller’s name, office, and callback number,
end the call, and check using CRA’s official
contact-verification tool or contact details
found independently on Canada.ca.

00:01:45.807 --> 00:01:55.610
CRA may request some information for a
legitimate identity check or missing tax
information, but never share passwords or
one-time security codes with an unexpected
caller.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:02.311
For the two fictional contacts, identify the
requested action, one warning cue, and the
verification route.

00:02:02.541 --> 00:02:03.726
Pause before choosing.

00:02:10.656 --> 00:02:14.734
For message A, the safe-account transfer and
code request are high-risk.

00:02:14.964 --> 00:02:18.626
Do not transfer or reply; contact the bank
independently.

00:02:18.856 --> 00:02:23.978
For message B, the notification may be
legitimate, but this fictional card is not
proof.

00:02:24.208 --> 00:02:28.048
Navigate to Canada.ca yourself and check the
account through the official route.

00:02:28.278 --> 00:02:34.023
If a caller claims to be CRA, remember that
CRA can call, but caller ID can be spoofed.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:39.046
Ask for callback details, end the call, and
verify independently if unsure.

00:02:39.276 --> 00:02:44.780
The lesson is neither “trust it” nor “all
contacts are fake.” It is pause, verify, then
act.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:57.977
If a learner already clicked or shared
information, the response changes: stop
interacting, secure the affected account from
a trusted device, and promptly contact the
relevant institution through an independently
found route.

00:02:58.207 --> 00:03:02.432
Preserve the message and time, but do not
reopen its link to collect evidence.

00:03:02.662 --> 00:03:09.081
If money was lost, contact local police
promptly and report through current Canadian
Anti-Fraud Centre guidance.

00:03:09.311 --> 00:03:11.375
Reporting does not guarantee recovery.

00:03:11.605 --> 00:03:17.326
A second person offering guaranteed
reimbursement for an upfront fee is another
reason to pause and verify.

